    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Logistic regression is the standard linear classifier: it computes a weighted sum of the features and squashes it through the sigmoid to get a probability.
z = w·x + b p = P(y = 1 | x) = σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e⁻ᶻ) log(p / (1 − p)) = z
The model is linear in the log-odds. Predict class 1 when p ≥ 0.5 — equivalently z ≥ 0 — so the decision boundary w·x + b = 0 is a straight line (hyperplane). The threshold can and often should be moved (see ROC, PR, Thresholds & Calibration).
import numpy as np

sigmoid = lambda z: 1 / (1 + np.exp(-z))
print(sigmoid(np.array([-4, -1, 0, 1, 4])).round(3))    # [0.018 0.269 0.5   0.731 0.982]
print(round(np.log(0.8 / 0.2), 3))                      # 1.386 ← logit: p = 0.8 means z ≈ 1.386
Training: maximum likelihood = minimum log-loss
J(w, b) = −(1/n) Σ [ yᵢ log pᵢ + (1 − yᵢ) log(1 − pᵢ) ] ∂J/∂w = (1/n) Σ (pᵢ − yᵢ) xᵢ
The cost is convex, so solvers (lbfgs by default) find the global minimum; there is no closed form. scikit-learn adds an L2 penalty by default (C=1.0, where smaller C = stronger regularisation), so scale your features.
