SELECT '6233dcca-6feb-7907-9aab-b893b69b4a66', '7d856739-932d-77f1-ae94-0b168d544f9f', 'Abstract Base Classes & Protocols', 'Abstract Base Classes & Protocols', 'Two ways to describe “what an object must be able to do”: ABCs check it by inheritance (nominal typing), Protocols by shape (structural typing — static duck typing).
abc.ABC and @abstractmethod
An abstract base class can''t be instantiated while it has abstract methods. Subclasses must implement every one of them — the error appears when you create an object, not when you call the missing method.
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Exporter(ABC):
    """Template method pattern: export() fixes the algorithm, subclasses fill in the steps."""

    def export(self, rows):
        return [self.header()] + [self.format_row(r) for r in rows]

    @abstractmethod
    def header(self) -> str: ...

    @abstractmethod
    def format_row(self, row) -> str: ...

class CsvExporter(Exporter):
    def header(self):
        return "name,score"
    def format_row(self, row):
        return f"{row[0]},{row[1]}"

class Broken(Exporter):
    def header(self):
        return "x"

print(CsvExporter().export([("asha", 91), ("ravi", 84)]))   # [''name,score'', ''asha,91'', ''ravi,84'']
try:
    Broken()
except TypeError as e:
    print(e)   # Can''t instantiate abstract class Broken without an implementation for abstract method ''format_row''
print(sorted(Exporter.__abstractmethods__))                  # [''format_row'', ''header'']
Abstract properties & class methods
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Shape(ABC):
    @property
    @abstractmethod                    # @abstractmethod must be the innermost decorator
    def sides(self) -> int: ...

    @classmethod
    @abstractmethod
    def unit(cls) -> "Shape": ...

    def describe(self):                # ABCs can hold ordinary, shared methods too
        return f"{type(self).__name__} with {self.sides} sides"

class Triangle(Shape):
    sides = 3                          # a plain class attribute satisfies the abstract property
    @classmethod
    def unit(cls):
        return cls()

print(Triangle.unit().describe())      # Triangle with 3 sides
Key point
An abstract method may still have a body; subclasses can call it with super().method() — handy for shared default behaviour that must be consciously opted into.
collections.abc: get methods for free
The ABCs in collections.abc are mixins: implement the few abstract methods and inherit the rest. Implement __getitem__ + __len__ and Sequence gives you in, iteration, reversed, index and count.
from collections.abc import Sequence

class Countdown(Sequence):
    def __init__(self, n):
        self.n = n
    def __len__(self):
        return self.n
    def __getitem__(self, i):
        if isinstance(i, slice):
            return [self[j] for j in range(*i.indices(len(self)))]
        if i < 0:
            i += self.n
        if not 0 <= i < self.n:
            raise IndexError(i)
        return self.n - i

c = Countdown(5)
print(list(c), c[-1], c[1:3])             # [5, 4, 3, 2, 1] 1 [4, 3]
print(3 in c, c.index(2), c.count(9))     # True 3 0
print(list(reversed(c)))                  # [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
ABC	You implement	You get for free
Iterable	__iter__	—
Iterator	__next__	__iter__
Sequence	__getitem__, __len__	__contains__, __iter__, __reversed__, index, count
MutableSequence	+ __setitem__, __delitem__, insert	append, extend, pop, remove, +=…
Mapping	__getitem__, __iter__, __len__	get, keys, items, values, ==, in
MutableMapping	+ __setitem__, __delitem__	pop, setdefault, update, clear…
Set	__contains__, __iter__, __len__	<=, |, &, -, ^, isdisjoint
isinstance with ABCs, register & __subclasshook__
from collections.abc import Iterable, Mapping, Sized, Hashable, Callable

print(isinstance([1], Iterable), isinstance({}, Mapping))      # True True
print(isinstance("abc", Hashable), isinstance([], Hashable))   # True False

class Bag:                     # doesn''t inherit from Sized...
    def __len__(self):
        return 0

print(isinstance(Bag(), Sized), isinstance(len, Callable))     # True True
Some ABCs (Sized, Iterable, Hashable, Callable…) check structurally through __subclasshook__: any class with the right method counts. You can also declare a virtual subclass with register.
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
import io

class Drawable(ABC):
    pass

class LegacyWidget:                    # third-party class you can''t modify
    def draw(self):
        return "drawn"

Drawable.register(LegacyWidget)        # a *virtual* subclass
print(isinstance(LegacyWidget(), Drawable), Drawable in LegacyWidget.__mro__)   # True False

class Closeable(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def close(self): ...

    @classmethod
    def __subclasshook__(cls, C):      # structural check: "has a close() method"
        if cls is Closeable:
            return any("close" in B.__dict__ for B in C.__mro__) or NotImplemented
        return NotImplemented

print(isinstance(io.StringIO(), Closeable), isinstance(42, Closeable))   # True False
Watch out
register is a promise, not a check — Python never verifies that the registered class really implements the methods.
typing.Protocol — structural typing
A Protocol lists the methods a type needs. Any class with those methods matches — no inheritance, no registration. Type checkers (mypy, pyright) verify it statically; @runtime_checkable also enables isinstance.
from typing import Protocol, runtime_checkable

@runtime_checkable
class SupportsArea(Protocol):
    def area(self) -> float: ...

class Square:                          # no mention of SupportsArea anywhere
    def __init__(self, s):
        self.s = s
    def area(self):
        return self.s ** 2

class Rect:
    def __init__(self, w, h):
        self.w, self.h = w, h
    def area(self):
        return self.w * self.h

def total_area(shapes: list[SupportsArea]) -> float:
    return sum(s.area() for s in shapes)

print(total_area([Square(2), Rect(2, 3)]))                     # 10
print(isinstance(Square(1), SupportsArea), isinstance("x", SupportsArea))   # True False
Watch out
A runtime isinstance check against a Protocol only checks that the method names exist — not their signatures or return types. Signature checking happens only in the type checker.
ABC or Protocol?
	ABC (abc.ABC)	Protocol (typing.Protocol)
Typing style	Nominal — must inherit or register	Structural — just have the methods
Enforced	At instantiation (TypeError)	By the static type checker
Shared code	Yes — concrete methods, template methods	Possible, but only for explicit subclasses
Works with third-party classes	Only via register	Automatically
Best for	Frameworks and class hierarchies you own	Library APIs, callbacks, “anything with .read()”
Do
Use an ABC when you own the hierarchy and want to share implementation; use a Protocol to describe what a function accepts from arbitrary callers.
Avoid
Creating an ABC for every class “just in case” — in Python, plain duck typing is often enough.
Interview angle
Why can''t you instantiate a class with abstract methods?
ABCMeta records unimplemented @abstractmethods in __abstractmethods__; object.__new__ refuses to create an instance while that set is non-empty. The subclass becomes concrete once it overrides all of them.
Nominal vs structural typing?
Nominal: a type matches only if it declares the relationship (inherits from the ABC). Structural: a type matches if it has the required members (Protocol). Python''s runtime is duck-typed; Protocols bring that duck typing to static type checkers.
What does collections.abc give you beyond isinstance checks?
Mixin methods. Subclass Sequence, Mapping or MutableSet, implement the handful of abstract methods, and you inherit a full, consistent API — the safe way to build a custom container instead of subclassing list or dict directly.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Abstract Base Classes & Protocols'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Abstract Base Classes & Protocols'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Two ways to describe “what an object must be able to do”: ABCs check it by inheritance (nominal typing), Protocols by shape (structural typing — static duck typing).
abc.ABC and @abstractmethod
An abstract base class can''t be instantiated while it has abstract methods. Subclasses must implement every one of them — the error appears when you create an object, not when you call the missing method.
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Exporter(ABC):
    """Template method pattern: export() fixes the algorithm, subclasses fill in the steps."""

    def export(self, rows):
        return [self.header()] + [self.format_row(r) for r in rows]

    @abstractmethod
    def header(self) -> str: ...

    @abstractmethod
    def format_row(self, row) -> str: ...

class CsvExporter(Exporter):
    def header(self):
        return "name,score"
    def format_row(self, row):
        return f"{row[0]},{row[1]}"

class Broken(Exporter):
    def header(self):
        return "x"

print(CsvExporter().export([("asha", 91), ("ravi", 84)]))   # [''name,score'', ''asha,91'', ''ravi,84'']
try:
    Broken()
except TypeError as e:
    print(e)   # Can''t instantiate abstract class Broken without an implementation for abstract method ''format_row''
print(sorted(Exporter.__abstractmethods__))                  # [''format_row'', ''header'']
Abstract properties & class methods
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Shape(ABC):
    @property
    @abstractmethod                    # @abstractmethod must be the innermost decorator
    def sides(self) -> int: ...

    @classmethod
    @abstractmethod
    def unit(cls) -> "Shape": ...

    def describe(self):                # ABCs can hold ordinary, shared methods too
        return f"{type(self).__name__} with {self.sides} sides"

class Triangle(Shape):
    sides = 3                          # a plain class attribute satisfies the abstract property
    @classmethod
    def unit(cls):
        return cls()

print(Triangle.unit().describe())      # Triangle with 3 sides
Key point
An abstract method may still have a body; subclasses can call it with super().method() — handy for shared default behaviour that must be consciously opted into.
collections.abc: get methods for free
The ABCs in collections.abc are mixins: implement the few abstract methods and inherit the rest. Implement __getitem__ + __len__ and Sequence gives you in, iteration, reversed, index and count.
from collections.abc import Sequence

class Countdown(Sequence):
    def __init__(self, n):
        self.n = n
    def __len__(self):
        return self.n
    def __getitem__(self, i):
        if isinstance(i, slice):
            return [self[j] for j in range(*i.indices(len(self)))]
        if i < 0:
            i += self.n
        if not 0 <= i < self.n:
            raise IndexError(i)
        return self.n - i

c = Countdown(5)
print(list(c), c[-1], c[1:3])             # [5, 4, 3, 2, 1] 1 [4, 3]
print(3 in c, c.index(2), c.count(9))     # True 3 0
print(list(reversed(c)))                  # [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
ABC	You implement	You get for free
Iterable	__iter__	—
Iterator	__next__	__iter__
Sequence	__getitem__, __len__	__contains__, __iter__, __reversed__, index, count
MutableSequence	+ __setitem__, __delitem__, insert	append, extend, pop, remove, +=…
Mapping	__getitem__, __iter__, __len__	get, keys, items, values, ==, in
MutableMapping	+ __setitem__, __delitem__	pop, setdefault, update, clear…
Set	__contains__, __iter__, __len__	<=, |, &, -, ^, isdisjoint
isinstance with ABCs, register & __subclasshook__
from collections.abc import Iterable, Mapping, Sized, Hashable, Callable

print(isinstance([1], Iterable), isinstance({}, Mapping))      # True True
print(isinstance("abc", Hashable), isinstance([], Hashable))   # True False

class Bag:                     # doesn''t inherit from Sized...
    def __len__(self):
        return 0

print(isinstance(Bag(), Sized), isinstance(len, Callable))     # True True
Some ABCs (Sized, Iterable, Hashable, Callable…) check structurally through __subclasshook__: any class with the right method counts. You can also declare a virtual subclass with register.
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
import io

class Drawable(ABC):
    pass

class LegacyWidget:                    # third-party class you can''t modify
    def draw(self):
        return "drawn"

Drawable.register(LegacyWidget)        # a *virtual* subclass
print(isinstance(LegacyWidget(), Drawable), Drawable in LegacyWidget.__mro__)   # True False

class Closeable(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def close(self): ...

    @classmethod
    def __subclasshook__(cls, C):      # structural check: "has a close() method"
        if cls is Closeable:
            return any("close" in B.__dict__ for B in C.__mro__) or NotImplemented
        return NotImplemented

print(isinstance(io.StringIO(), Closeable), isinstance(42, Closeable))   # True False
Watch out
register is a promise, not a check — Python never verifies that the registered class really implements the methods.
typing.Protocol — structural typing
A Protocol lists the methods a type needs. Any class with those methods matches — no inheritance, no registration. Type checkers (mypy, pyright) verify it statically; @runtime_checkable also enables isinstance.
from typing import Protocol, runtime_checkable

@runtime_checkable
class SupportsArea(Protocol):
    def area(self) -> float: ...

class Square:                          # no mention of SupportsArea anywhere
    def __init__(self, s):
        self.s = s
    def area(self):
        return self.s ** 2

class Rect:
    def __init__(self, w, h):
        self.w, self.h = w, h
    def area(self):
        return self.w * self.h

def total_area(shapes: list[SupportsArea]) -> float:
    return sum(s.area() for s in shapes)

print(total_area([Square(2), Rect(2, 3)]))                     # 10
print(isinstance(Square(1), SupportsArea), isinstance("x", SupportsArea))   # True False
Watch out
A runtime isinstance check against a Protocol only checks that the method names exist — not their signatures or return types. Signature checking happens only in the type checker.
ABC or Protocol?
	ABC (abc.ABC)	Protocol (typing.Protocol)
Typing style	Nominal — must inherit or register	Structural — just have the methods
Enforced	At instantiation (TypeError)	By the static type checker
Shared code	Yes — concrete methods, template methods	Possible, but only for explicit subclasses
Works with third-party classes	Only via register	Automatically
Best for	Frameworks and class hierarchies you own	Library APIs, callbacks, “anything with .read()”
Do
Use an ABC when you own the hierarchy and want to share implementation; use a Protocol to describe what a function accepts from arbitrary callers.
Avoid
Creating an ABC for every class “just in case” — in Python, plain duck typing is often enough.
Interview angle
Why can''t you instantiate a class with abstract methods?
ABCMeta records unimplemented @abstractmethods in __abstractmethods__; object.__new__ refuses to create an instance while that set is non-empty. The subclass becomes concrete once it overrides all of them.
Nominal vs structural typing?
Nominal: a type matches only if it declares the relationship (inherits from the ABC). Structural: a type matches if it has the required members (Protocol). Python''s runtime is duck-typed; Protocols bring that duck typing to static type checkers.
What does collections.abc give you beyond isinstance checks?
Mixin methods. Subclass Sequence, Mapping or MutableSet, implement the handful of abstract methods, and you inherit a full, consistent API — the safe way to build a custom container instead of subclassing list or dict directly.'), 'C'),
