SELECT '5ece1f24-31e5-7af2-aa47-777becea6e56', 'c764b86d-4f95-7c85-aa65-80d585ef8591', 'Inheritance, MRO & super()', 'Inheritance, MRO & super()', 'A subclass reuses and specialises its parent. Python supports multiple inheritance and resolves methods with a predictable order — the MRO.
Inheritance & overriding
class Animal:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
    def speak(self):
        return "..."
    def describe(self):
        return f"{self.name} says {self.speak()}"

class Dog(Animal):
    def __init__(self, name, breed):
        super().__init__(name)           # let the parent set up its part
        self.breed = breed
    def speak(self):                     # override
        return "Woof"

d = Dog("Rex", "lab")
print(d.describe())                              # Rex says Woof
print(isinstance(d, Animal), issubclass(Dog, Animal))   # True True
Key point
describe lives in Animal but calls self.speak() — Python looks it up on the actual object, so the Dog version runs. That''s polymorphism.
Multiple inheritance & the MRO
MRO (Method Resolution Order) — the order in which classes are searched for an attribute, computed with C3 linearization. See it with Class.__mro__ or Class.mro().
class A:
    def hello(self): return "A"
class B(A):
    def hello(self): return "B→" + super().hello()
class C(A):
    def hello(self): return "C→" + super().hello()
class D(B, C):
    def hello(self): return "D→" + super().hello()

print(D().hello())                          # D→B→C→A
print([k.__name__ for k in D.__mro__])      # [''D'', ''B'', ''C'', ''A'', ''object'']
Watch out
super() means “the next class in the MRO”, not “my parent”. In D(B, C), B''s super() goes to C, not A — that''s what makes cooperative multiple inheritance work.
Abstract base classes
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Shape(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def area(self) -> float: ...

class Square(Shape):
    def __init__(self, side):
        self.side = side
    def area(self):
        return self.side ** 2

print(Square(3).area())          # 9
try:
    Shape()
except TypeError:
    print("can''t instantiate")   # can''t instantiate
Duck typing & composition
Duck typing
“If it walks like a duck…” — code relies on what an object can do (has read(), is iterable) rather than its class.
Composition over inheritance
Hold other objects as attributes (self.engine = Engine()) instead of building deep class trees.
Mixins
Small classes adding one capability (e.g. JsonMixin.to_json) combined through multiple inheritance.
Protocols
typing.Protocol describes the methods a type needs — static duck typing for type checkers.',
