SELECT 'eadbd4fc-4cd0-7449-9cbb-509a79d056b1', '59b07199-90a7-785d-8951-9c01e4f89508', 'Memory, Mutability & Garbage Collection', 'Memory, Mutability & Garbage Collection', 'Variables are references, objects live on the heap, and CPython frees them with reference counting plus a cycle collector. Mutability explains most “weird” Python bugs.
Names point to objects
a = [1, 2]
b = a                    # b refers to the SAME list
b.append(3)
print(a, a is b)         # [1, 2, 3] True

x = 5
y = x
y += 1                   # ints are immutable: y now points to a new object
print(x, y)              # 5 6
Immutable (can''t change in place)	Mutable (can change in place)
int, float, bool, str, bytes	list, dict, set, bytearray
tuple, frozenset, None	Most user-defined objects
Key point
A tuple is immutable, but if it contains a list, that list can still change: t = ([1],); t[0].append(2) works.
+= differs for mutable and immutable types
nums = [1]
alias = nums
nums += [2]              # list: extends in place
print(alias)             # [1, 2]

s = "a"
alias_s = s
s += "b"                 # str: builds a new object
print(alias_s, s)        # a ab
Reference counting & the cycle collector
Reference counting
Every object tracks how many references point to it. When the count hits 0, it is freed immediately.
Cycle detection
Objects that reference each other (a ↔ b) never reach 0. The gc module periodically finds and frees such unreachable cycles.
Generations
New objects are checked often; objects that survive are promoted and checked less — most objects die young.
import sys, gc

data = []
# getrefcount''s own argument adds a temporary reference:
print(sys.getrefcount(data) >= 2)   # True

class Node:
    def __init__(self):
        self.other = None
a, b = Node(), Node()
a.other, b.other = b, a             # a reference cycle
del a, b
print(gc.collect() >= 2)            # True
Memory tips
Generators over lists
Stream data instead of materialising it: sum(x for x in …).
__slots__
Drop per-instance __dict__s when creating millions of objects.
sys.getsizeof
Shallow size of one object in bytes — it doesn''t include what it references.
Interning
Short identifier-like strings and small ints are reused. Never depend on it; compare with ==.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Memory, Mutability & Garbage Collection'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Memory, Mutability & Garbage Collection'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Variables are references, objects live on the heap, and CPython frees them with reference counting plus a cycle collector. Mutability explains most “weird” Python bugs.
Names point to objects
a = [1, 2]
b = a                    # b refers to the SAME list
b.append(3)
print(a, a is b)         # [1, 2, 3] True

x = 5
y = x
y += 1                   # ints are immutable: y now points to a new object
print(x, y)              # 5 6
Immutable (can''t change in place)	Mutable (can change in place)
int, float, bool, str, bytes	list, dict, set, bytearray
tuple, frozenset, None	Most user-defined objects
Key point
A tuple is immutable, but if it contains a list, that list can still change: t = ([1],); t[0].append(2) works.
+= differs for mutable and immutable types
nums = [1]
alias = nums
nums += [2]              # list: extends in place
print(alias)             # [1, 2]

s = "a"
alias_s = s
s += "b"                 # str: builds a new object
print(alias_s, s)        # a ab
Reference counting & the cycle collector
Reference counting
Every object tracks how many references point to it. When the count hits 0, it is freed immediately.
Cycle detection
Objects that reference each other (a ↔ b) never reach 0. The gc module periodically finds and frees such unreachable cycles.
Generations
New objects are checked often; objects that survive are promoted and checked less — most objects die young.
import sys, gc

data = []
# getrefcount''s own argument adds a temporary reference:
print(sys.getrefcount(data) >= 2)   # True

class Node:
    def __init__(self):
        self.other = None
a, b = Node(), Node()
a.other, b.other = b, a             # a reference cycle
del a, b
print(gc.collect() >= 2)            # True
